  <header class="qa-header">
    <p class="qa-pillar">Question · Norse Lore</p>
    <h1>What Are Odin's Ravens Called?</h1>
  </header>

  <section class="qa-snippet" aria-label="Direct answer">
    <p><strong class="claim-lore">[lore]</strong> Odin's two ravens are called <strong>Huginn</strong> (Old Norse: <em>Huginn</em>, meaning "Thought") and <strong>Muninn</strong> (Old Norse: <em>Muninn</em>, meaning "Memory"). Their names are given in <em>Grímnismál</em>, stanza 20 — the only surviving Old Norse verse that names both ravens together. The poem is preserved in the <em>Codex Regius</em> (c. 1270 CE).</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>The Source: Grímnismál Stanza 20</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-lore">[lore]</strong> The names Huginn and Muninn appear in the Old Norse Eddic poem <em>Grímnismál</em> ("The Sayings of Grímnir"), stanza 20:</p>
    <blockquote class="qa-quote">
      <p lang="non">"Huginn ok Muninn / fljúga hverjan dag / Jörmungrund yfir…"</p>
      <footer>"Huginn and Muninn fly every day over the vast earth…" — <em>Grímnismál</em> st. 20, Codex Regius</footer>
    </blockquote>
    <p>Snorri Sturluson's <em>Prose Edda</em> (c. 1220 CE), <em>Gylfaginning</em> chapter 38, confirms the names and adds that the ravens sit on Odin's shoulders and return each morning with news of the world.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>What the Names Mean</h2>
    <dl class="qa-dl">
      <div>
        <dt><strong>Huginn</strong></dt>
        <dd>From Old Norse <em>hugr</em> = "thought", "mind", or "intention". Universally translated as "Thought".</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt><strong>Muninn</strong></dt>
        <dd>From Old Norse <em>muna</em> = "to remember" or <em>munr</em> = "mind/memory/desire". Most often translated as "Memory", but some scholars (including Anthony Faulkes) note "desire" or "longing" as alternate meanings.</dd>
      </div>
    </dl>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Odin's Fear for Muninn</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-lore">[lore]</strong> The stanza continues: "I fear for Huginn that he may not return, yet I fear more for Muninn." This line has fascinated scholars — why does Odin fear more for Memory than for Thought? The most cited interpretation is that memory, once lost, cannot be regained: thought can begin again, but memory is irreplaceable. See: <a href="/lore/norse/grimnismal-stanza-20/">Grímnismál Stanza 20 — full analysis</a>.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Further Reading</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><a href="/lore/norse/huginn-and-muninn/">Huginn and Muninn — full analysis of the texts</a></li>
      <li><a href="/lore/norse/odin-the-raven-god/">Odin: the Raven-God (Hrafnaguð)</a></li>
      <li><a href="/lore/norse/grimnismal-stanza-20/">Grímnismál Stanza 20 — Old Norse text with gloss</a></li>
      <li><a href="/species/common-raven/">Common Raven — the bird behind the myth</a></li>
    </ul>
  </section>

